  "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B017",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B024",
    "B014"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "I change my preferred configuration from B024 to B017. Optical lattice clocks rest on a physically sharp idea: the magic-wavelength lattice makes it possible to interrogate many atoms while suppressing the otherwise decisive trapping shift. Katori's originating concept and Ye's foundational experimental realization form a compact, credible attribution. The method has been independently reproduced and has become a durable precision platform for both metrology and fundamental measurements. Its merit does not depend on a future redefinition of the second; the essential scientific achievement is already mature.",
    "case_against": "The committee must distinguish the invention and realization of the optical-lattice-clock method from the extensive later work that produced present-day performance. A citation should not suggest that the two laureates alone are responsible for every technical advance or application. The historical record should also establish the distinct foundational character of each proposed laureate's contribution.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that the magic-wavelength architecture, repeated independent demonstrations, and the distinct roles of Katori and Ye make optical lattice clocks a mature and clearly attributable achievement.",
        "response": "I agree, especially with the separation between the original method and the later collective development of optical-clock capability. From an atomic-physics perspective, the suppression of lattice-induced shifts is a genuine conceptual and experimental breakthrough, not merely an incremental gain in precision. The evidence of reproducibility makes this a robust physical achievement."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson favors B017 because it has a unified concept-to-realization history and a more straightforward two-person attribution than B024, while warning that later contributions must be bounded carefully.",
        "response": "This comparison has changed my judgment. I still regard B024 as one of the deepest quantum insights on the shortlist, but its proposed configuration joins two historically distinct discoveries. B017 offers a more coherent achievement without sacrificing fundamental value: an advance in controlling quantum systems that has opened a new regime of measurement."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig supports B024 for its enduring insight into observable quantum phase, while recognizing that its joint citation must not obscure distinct priority histories.",
        "response": "I share his scientific admiration for B024 and retain it as my first alternative. But the unresolved question is structural, not a minor wording issue: the Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase have different origins, and the two-person combined configuration risks making one achievement appear auxiliary to the other. B017 is the more defensible final public configuration."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the historical documentation can establish the boundary between Katori and Ye's foundational contributions and later essential optical-clock advances with sufficient precision.",
      "Whether B024 could instead be reformulated around one coherent achievement without omitting indispensable contributors or distorting its distinct histories."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
